## Test Smell Audit — Academic Catalog (testsmells.org)

**Target:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · MSTest 4.1 · .NET 10  
**Integration-test calibration:** Applied — class is annotated `[TestCategory("Integration")]`

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### ❶ Conditional Test Logic — High

**Smell:** Control flow (`if`/`else`) inside a test determines which assertion runs — one branch may never execute, hiding gaps.  
**Location:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 94–109)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** The `PremiumUser` branch may *never* execute (Eve is inserted as a regular `User`), giving false confidence that premium logic is covered.

---

#### ❷ Sleepy Test — High

**Smell:** `Thread.Sleep` introduces non-deterministic timing and slows the suite.  
**Location:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // 3 seconds of waste per run
```

**Integration-test calibration note:** Even in integration tests, a hard sleep is a smell — it slows CI and may flake under load.

**Fix — poll/retry or use an async event:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll with timeout instead of fixed sleep
    var sent = await WaitUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(sent);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** 3 s of dead time per run; flaky failures on slow agents; compounds across parallel suites.

---

#### ❸ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — High

**Smell:** Test exercises code but never asserts anything — passes even if every insert silently fails.  
**Location:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion
}
```

**Fix — assert observable outcome:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Gives false coverage — 100% pass rate regardless of whether data is actually persisted.

---

#### ❹ Assertion Roulette — Medium

**Smell:** Multiple assertions with no explanatory message — when one fails, MSTest reports a line number but no semantic context.  
**Location:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–39)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Fix — add message parameters:**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated ID should be positive");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** CI failure output shows `Assert.AreEqual failed` with no clue which property failed without opening the file.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✓

These tests are well-structured — each has a clear Arrange/Act/Assert, tests a single behavior, and uses contextually obvious values:

| Test | Why it's clean |
|------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Single behavior, clear assertion |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Minimal assertions, intent obvious |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count `3` is self-documenting (3 inserts above) |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Good async pattern, clear assertion |

The use of an **in-memory SQLite** database is excellent — it avoids the Mystery Guest smell entirely while keeping real SQL semantics.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix Effort | Impact |
|----------|-------|-----------|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | ~2 min | Eliminates false-pass risk |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | ~5 min | Eliminates hidden dead branch |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | ~10 min | Removes 3 s from CI, fixes flakiness |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | ~3 min | Improves failure diagnostics |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 8 tests are clean. The 3 high-severity smells (Conditional Logic, Sleepy, Assertion-Free) are the ones most correlated with flaky/misleading results in the literature (Camara et al., SAST 2021). Fix those first — they're all quick wins.
